# vibe content-guard allowlist.
#
# WHITELIST MODEL (Martin, 2026-08-13): pseudo-PII that is deliberately usable
# anywhere in this repo is whitelisted below by explicit pattern; anything
# else stays guarded. Content entries are whole-line ERE matches, so keep each
# pattern anchored to a literal that is itself pseudo-PII (reserved example
# domains / the public product address) — never a plain word that could appear
# on a line beside something real. Caveat carried from the path-warn design:
# a content ERE suppresses every finding on a matching line, so never let a
# whitelisted literal share a line with anything sensitive.
#
# Emails at IANA-reserved documentation domains (example.com / *.example):
.*@example\.com.*
.*@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.example([^A-Za-z0-9-].*)?
# The public product contact address (deliberately published):
.*time@andeye\.com.*
#
# path-warn entries: WARN-tier rules (emails, IPs, home paths, mdns) are
# skipped for these files' staged/range diffs. BLOCK-tier secret rules ALWAYS
# still fire on every file - a path-warn can never mask a real secret.
# Checks fixtures are dense with reserved-domain synthetic identities; the
# committed /vss session audits quote the guard's own categories when
# describing what they verified.
path-warn:Sources/timeandeyeChecks/*
path-warn:.vss/*
# The two guard-config files themselves: gitignore entries like the one for
# vibe's settings.local.json runtime config false-positive the hostname WARN
# on the ".local" substring (filenames, not hostnames). WARN-off for these
# structural files only; BLOCK-tier secret rules still fire on them.
path-warn:.gitignore
path-warn:.vibe-content-allow
